<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title><%= (book.meta.short || book.season.code).toUpperCase() %> · MARGIN</title>
<meta name="description" content="A reading log.">
<%# The catalogue links its own stylesheet only. margin.css is a different
    object's palette and would fight this one at every token. %>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/fonts.css?v=<%= v.fonts %>">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/lookbook.css?v=<%= v.css %>">
<link rel="icon" href='data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 32 32"><rect width="32" height="32" fill="%230A0A0A"/><text x="16" y="22" font-family="Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="500" text-anchor="middle" fill="%23E8E4DC">AW</text></svg>'>
<% if (locals.robots) { %><meta name="robots" content="<%= robots %>"><% } %>

<%#
  ── THE LINK, WHEN IT IS PASTED ───────────────────────────

  This page has its own <head> rather than the shared partial, so it emitted
  no preview metadata at all: the best object the product makes arrived in a
  chat window as a bare grey rectangle with a URL under it.

  The poster IS the preview. Nothing here is a caption or a share string —
  §8 refuses those, because an artifact that needs a sentence appended to it
  is not the artifact this one is.
%>
<% if (locals.ogImage) { %>
<meta property="og:title" content="<%= book.meta.short || book.season.code.toUpperCase() %> — a reading season">
<meta property="og:type" content="article">
<meta property="og:image" content="<%= ogImage %>">
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1080">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="1920">
<% if (locals.ogDescription) { %><meta property="og:description" content="<%= ogDescription %>"><% } %>
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<meta name="twitter:image" content="<%= ogImage %>">
<% } %>
</head>
<body class="lookbook-page">

<%#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  THE SEASONAL LOOKBOOK — §02 RUNNING ORDER
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  §00 — "A lookbook is not a summary. It is a catalogue."

  Nothing on this page congratulates, nothing addresses the reader as "you",
  and there is no confetti, no superlative and no exclamation mark. §01 —
  "No emoji anywhere in the artifact, including share cards."
%>
<%#
  The grain. One SVG turbulence over the whole page at low opacity, the way
  a printed catalogue is never quite flat. It is the only decoration in the
  feature and it is fixed, so scrolling moves the page under it rather than
  carrying it along.
%>
<svg class="lb-grain" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true">
  <filter id="lbgrain"><feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency=".82" numOctaves="3"/></filter>
  <rect width="100%" height="100%" filter="url(#lbgrain)"/>
</svg>

<main class="lookbook" id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'lb', book.season.code,
        '--bruise:' + h.css.color(book.bruise) +
        (book.accent ? ';--accent:' + h.css.color(book.accent) : '')) %>">

  <%# ── FRONT MATTER — COVER ─────────────────────────────
      "Season code only, set large. Archive number beneath it — permanent,
      citable, collectible. The user's name does not go on the cover. It
      goes in the credits, like a house does." %>
  <%# The archive number — SS26-0045-A — is gone from the cover, the line
      sheet and the colophon. It was styled as permanent, citable and
      collectible, and it is none of those: it is a reference number for a
      catalogue of one person's own reading, cited by nobody. The season
      code says which season this is, which is the only identification
      anybody needed. %>
  <header class="lb-cover lb-assemble">
    <h1 class="lb-code"><%= (book.meta.short || book.season.code).toUpperCase().replace(/^(SS|AW)/, '$1 ') %></h1>
    <span class="lb-bruise-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </header>

  <%#
    ── THE DESCENT ─────────────────────────────────────────

    The show note, alone, in the dark, at reading size.

    It used to sit in a twelve-column grid beside a label, which made an
    eighty-word statement look like a caption on a table. A house prints the
    note on its own page and lets you slow down for it, and the ground goes
    darker underneath because that is what the reader is being asked to do.

    The two hairlines are the only ornament: a shaft, masked at both ends,
    which is what a column of type standing alone in a dark field already
    looks like.
  %>
  <% if (book.showNotes) { %>
    <section class="lb-descent">
      <span class="lb-shaft" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></span>
      <div class="lb-note lb-assemble">
        <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">SHOW NOTE</div>
        <p class="lb-note-body"><%= book.showNotes %></p>
        <%# Whose sentence this is. §04's habit of citing applies to the
            house's own copy: a note composed from counts says so, and one
            the reader wrote is signed. %>
        <%# WRITE IT YOURSELF → pointed at the season page's note editor,
            which has been removed. The citation stays: whose sentence this
            is remains worth saying. %>
        <div class="lb-cite">
          <%= book.noteIsTheirs ? 'WRITTEN BY THIS ACCOUNT' : 'COMPOSED FROM THE SEASON\'S OWN FIGURES' %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  <% } %>

  <%#
    ── THE SEASON, READ ACROSS ───────────────────────────

    One paragraph about the season as a whole, drawn from the composition
    histories rather than from the plots: books cut by editors, books held
    back from reviewers, a writer working in the shadow of another.

    It sits HERE, at the front, and that is a change from where it started.
    A synthesis at the foot of a long catalogue is a conclusion nobody
    reaches; at the front it is a way in, and every plate below is then read
    against it. Fashion houses put the argument before the clothes for the
    same reason.

    This is the only place in the catalogue written in the second person and
    the only one allowed a voice. It earns the exception by being the one
    piece that reads across the season instead of describing a single
    object, and it is held to a plainness rule the rest of the page is not:
    one point, in words a reader could repeat to somebody else.

    Absent until there are at least two documented histories to draw a line
    between. That absence is correct rather than a hole.
  %>
  <%# The section runs whenever the season has books in it. The POSTER used
      to live inside the note's condition, so a season with too few colours
      to read across lost its poster as well — A/W 25 has two of seven and
      showed nothing here at all, when two derived bands and five hatched is
      exactly the picture it should be showing. %>
  <% if (book.looks.length) { %>
    <section class="lb-closing lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-closing-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">THE SEASON, READ ACROSS</div>
          <% if (!(book.closingNote && book.closingNote.note)) { %>
            <%# Not an error and not an apology: the arithmetic simply is not
                there yet, and the sentence says which arithmetic. %>
            <p class="lb-closing-body"><%= book.closingNote.shortfall %></p>
          <% } %>
          <%# The note comes back in one or two paragraphs. Printed through a
              single <p> the blank line collapsed and two distinct moves — the
              observation, then the claim drawn from it — ran together as one
              block. Split on the break, escaped per paragraph. %>
          <% if (book.closingNote && book.closingNote.note) { %>
          <p class="lb-closing-body"><%= book.closingNote.note %></p>
          <%# The provenance line says how many books the note speaks for and
              how many it does not. A note that counted the unwritten ones
              would be speaking for silence. %>
          <div class="lb-cite"><%= book.closingNote.provenance %></div>

          <%# The arithmetic itself, so a reader can check the sentence
              rather than take it. Every claim above is one of these counted. %>
          <details class="lb-drawn">
            <summary class="lb-cite">THE COUNT →</summary>
            <ul class="lb-drawn-list">
              <% book.closingNote.drawnFrom.forEach(function(d) { %>
                <li>
                  <span class="lb-drawn-pct"><%= d.pct %>%</span>
                  <span class="lb-drawn-emotion"><%= d.emotion %></span>
                  <span class="lb-drawn-title"><%= d.title %></span>
                </li>
              <% }) %>
            </ul>
          </details>
          <% } %>
        </div>

        <%#
          THE POSTER, beside it.

          Not an illustration of the note and not decoration: the season's
          own colours, sampled from the jackets of the books it is about, in
          bands weighted by how many books carried each one. Nobody
          art-directed it, which is the whole reason no two seasons look
          alike, and it is the same palette the printable poster is built
          from — this is that object, on the page, rather than a link to it.
        %>
        <% if (book.looks.length) { %>
          <figure class="lb-poster">
            <%# The same picture the printed sheet makes: ONE BAND PER BOOK,
                in reading order.

                It used to draw `colourway` — the distinct colours, weighted
                by how many books shared each — which came out as two bands
                for a seven-book season and was captioned "2 COLOURS FROM 7
                JACKETS". That was the jacket-extraction path, and it both
                collapsed the sequence and named the wrong source. A season
                of seven books is seven bands.

                A book with no derived colour keeps its band and shows the
                hatch. It is not filled in from the jacket: §05 forbids
                substituting a colour a book did not earn, and a poster that
                quietly fills its gaps is a poster that lies about what was
                read. %>
            <div class="lb-poster-field">
              <div class="lb-poster-strip">
                <% book.looks.forEach(function(l) { %>
                  <span class="lb-poster-band<%= l.colour && l.colour.source === 'derived' ? '' : ' band--none' %>"
                        <% if (l.colour && l.colour.source === 'derived') { %>id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'lbp', l.colour.hex,
                              'background:' + h.css.color(l.colour.hex)) %>"<% } %>></span>
                <% }) %>
              </div>
              <div class="lb-poster-foot">
                <span><%= (book.meta.short || book.season.code).toUpperCase().replace(/^(SS|AW)/, '$1 ') %></span>
                <span>MARGIN.CO</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption class="lb-cite lb-poster-cap">
              <%# The caption says what is actually on the strip. %>
              <% var drawn = book.looks.filter(function(l){ return l.colour && l.colour.source === 'derived'; }).length; %>
              <span>
                <%= drawn %> DERIVED <%= drawn === 1 ? 'COLOUR' : 'COLOURS' %>
                ACROSS <%= book.looks.length %> <%= book.looks.length === 1 ? 'BOOK' : 'BOOKS' %><%
                if (book.looks.length - drawn) { %> · <%= book.looks.length - drawn %> UNASSIGNED<% } %>
              </span>
              <% if (owner) { %>
                <a class="lb-cite" href="/season/<%= book.season.code %>/poster.png">PRINTABLE POSTER →</a>
              <% } %>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        <% } %>
      </div>
    </section>
  <% } %>

  <%# ── THE RUN OF SHOW ─────────────────────────────────
      "Runway order is chronological so the season has a shape — a
      beginning, a difficult middle, a close."

      Every look gets the same plates. There was a hero for a while, given a
      spread of its own while the rest held the line — but a season is not
      one book and eight also-rans, and the plate that made the difference
      was the wearing, which is derivable for all of them. %>
  <div class="lb-run-head lb-assemble">
    <div class="lb-label">THE RUN OF SHOW</div>
    <div class="lb-data"><%= book.looks.length %> LOOKS · IN THE ORDER THEY WERE FINISHED</div>
  </div>
  <% book.looks.forEach(function(l) { %>
    <article class="lb-look lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-grid">
        <div class="lb-c12">
          <div class="lb-look-no">LOOK <%= String(l.n).padStart(2, '0') %> / <%= String(l.of).padStart(2, '0') %></div>
          <h2 class="lb-look-title"><%= l.work.title %></h2>
          <% if (l.work.author) { %><p class="lb-look-author"><%= l.work.author %></p><% } %>
          <hr class="lb-rule">
        </div>
      </div>

      <%#
        The plates row has its own grid rather than the twelve-column one.
        On twelve columns the jacket cell was 346px holding a 255px plate,
        so the book sat at the same left margin as the title with ninety
        pixels of dead space after it — while the two text columns were
        jammed twenty-four apart and read as one block.

        Here the jacket column is exactly the jacket, it hangs into the
        outer margin the way a plate does in a catalogue, and the gutter
        between the columns is a gutter.
      %>
      <div class="lb-plates">
        <%# a. LOOK PLATE — "The cover as garment, styled: where it was
            read, how fast, what sat next to it on the shelf." %>
        <div class="lb-plates-jacket">
          <div class="lb-plate">
            <%#
              A missing jacket is a designed plate, not a hole.

              Everywhere else in the product a cover that is absent degrades
              to a galley — the title in the display face over the author on
              a tinted ground — and it is a nicer object than several of the
              real jackets. This page, the showpiece, was the one place that
              degraded to an empty bordered rectangle instead.

              The plate renders underneath and the jacket layers over it, so
              a cover that 404s at request time falls back to the same
              object rather than to a broken-image icon, and neither state
              shifts the layout.
            %>
            <span class="lb-jacket-plate">
              <span class="lb-jacket-galley" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="lb-galley-title"><%= h.shortTitle(l.work.title, { max: 60 }) %></span>
                <span class="lb-galley-author"><%= (l.work.author || '').toUpperCase() %></span>
              </span>
              <% if (l.edition && (l.edition.cover_cache_key || l.edition.cover_url)) { %>
                <img class="lb-jacket" src="<%= h.coverSrc(l.edition) %>" alt="" loading="lazy" data-cover>
              <% } %>
            </span>

            <div class="lb-styling lb-data">
              <% if (l.edition && l.edition.publisher) { %>
                <div><span>HOUSE</span><span><%= l.edition.publisher.toUpperCase().slice(0, 30) %></span></div>
              <% } %>
              <% if (l.edition && l.edition.page_count) { %>
                <div><span>YARDAGE</span><span><%= l.edition.page_count %>PP</span></div>
              <% } %>
              <%# Labelled for what the column actually holds. A good part
                  of the catalogue records an edition year here, and calling
                  that a first edition would be a small confident lie of
                  exactly the kind §04 is about. %>
              <% if (l.work.first_published_year) { %>
                <div><span>CATALOGUED</span><span><%= l.work.first_published_year %></span></div>
              <% } %>
              <% if (l.reading.finished_at) { %>
                <div><span>WORN</span><span><%= String(l.reading.finished_at).slice(0, 10) %></span></div>
              <% } %>
              <% if (l.translated) { %>
                <div><span>PATTERN</span><span>TRANSLATED</span></div>
              <% } %>
              <% if (l.reading.stars != null) { %>
                <div><span>GRADE</span><span><%= l.reading.stars %></span></div>
              <% } %>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <%#
          b. THE OBJECT'S HISTORY

          The conditions the book was written under: where the author
          physically was, what was happening around them, what they were
          reacting against, and how it got into print. Treated as exhibition
          catalogue copy — flat, past tense, no plot, no judgement.

          Every card carries its provenance, and the two kinds look
          different on purpose. A WRITTEN card was generated from retrieved
          text and passed a validator that checks every date, figure and
          name against the sources; it cites the article it came from. A
          MATERIAL card is a short list of facts the catalogue can prove
          about the object, shown when no composition history is documented
          — which is most contemporary and genre fiction.

          The thin card is set thin deliberately. It should not be mistaken
          at a glance for the other thing.
        %>
        <div class="lb-plates-read">
          <% if (l.history && l.history.kind === 'written') { %>
            <div class="lb-hist">
              <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">WRITTEN</div>
              <p class="lb-hist-body"><%= l.history.body %></p>
              <div class="lb-cite">
                <% if (l.history.edited) { %>
                  CORRECTED BY HAND
                <% } else if (l.history.source) { %>
                  AFTER <%= l.history.source.title.toUpperCase() %> · EN.WIKIPEDIA.ORG
                <% } else { %>
                  FROM RETRIEVED SOURCES
                <% } %>
              </div>
            </div>
          <% } else if (l.history && l.history.kind === 'material') { %>
            <div class="lb-hist lb-hist--thin">
              <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">THE OBJECT</div>
              <ul class="lb-hist-facts">
                <% l.history.facts.forEach(function(f) { %><li><%= f %></li><% }) %>
              </ul>
              <div class="lb-cite">NO COMPOSITION HISTORY ON RECORD</div>
            </div>
          <% } %>


          <%# THE WEARING. The session log as a run of show, and the point of
              it is the GAPS — a book set down for eleven days and picked up
              again is the most interesting thing the log knows, and a chart
              of cumulative pages hides it completely.

              It sits under WRITTEN now rather than in a column of its own.
              The book block is three columns: the object, what is known
              about it, and how its colour was arrived at. A fourth column
              left the derivation squeezed and the row ragged. %>
          <div class="lb-wearing">
            <% if (l.wearing.length) { %>
              <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">THE WEARING</div>
              <div class="lb-ledger">
                <% l.wearing.forEach(function(r) { %>
                  <div class="lb-led-row lb-led--<%= r.kind %>">
                    <span class="lb-led-when lb-data"><%= r.on %></span>
                    <span class="lb-led-what"><%= r.text %></span>
                  </div>
                <% }) %>
              </div>
              <div class="lb-cite">SESSION LOG · THIS ACCOUNT</div>
            <% } else if (l.held) { %>
              <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">THE WEARING</div>
              <p class="lb-cite">HELD <%= l.held %> DAYS. NO SESSIONS LOGGED.</p>
            <% } %>
          </div>

          <%# MARGINALIA is a second plate, not a continuation of the log. It
              is the only content on the page nobody can derive. %>
          <% if (owner && l.marginalia.length) { %>
            <div class="lb-plate-sec">
              <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">MARGINALIA</div>
              <% l.marginalia.forEach(function(m) { %>
                <blockquote class="lb-marg">
                  <% if (m.at) { %><cite class="lb-data"><%= m.at %></cite><% } %>
                  <p><%= m.text %></p>
                </blockquote>
              <% }) %>
            </div>
          <% } %>
        </div>

        <%#
          WHY THIS COLOUR.

          The derivation, shown rather than explained. Every value on this
          panel is already in the database: the anchors the blend was made
          from, their weights, the resulting hex, the generated name, and
          each component's own cited span and section.

          NOTHING HERE IS WRITTEN. There is no sentence about why oxidised
          carmine suits a book — that would be unsourced inference, which §06
          bans and which the entire citation apparatus exists to prevent. The
          panel is auditable arithmetic, and being able to follow it is what
          makes the colour read as considered rather than asserted.

          Absent entirely when a book has no colour: an empty derivation
          beside a hatch is worse than nothing.
        %>
        <% if (l.colour && l.colour.source === 'derived') { %>
          <div class="lb-plates-why">
            <%# The label sits ABOVE the panel, the way every other column
                head on this page does. Inside the border it read as a
                caption on a card. %>
            <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">WHY THIS COLOUR</div>
            <div class="lb-panel">

              <%# 1. What went in. Each row is anchor, name, share — and the
                  name carries both halves, so a reader sees Carmine, Soft
                  going in and Carmine, Oxidised coming out. %>
              <ol class="lb-why-anchors">
                <% l.colour.components.forEach(function(c) { %>
                  <li class="lb-why-anchor">
                    <i class="lb-why-chip"
                       id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'wa', c.anchorHex,
                             'background:' + h.css.color(c.anchorHex)) %>"></i>
                    <span class="lb-why-nm"><%= c.emotion %> · <%= c.name %></span>
                    <span class="lb-why-w"><%= Math.round(c.weight * 100) %>%</span>
                  </li>
                <% }) %>
              </ol>

              <div class="lb-why-arrow" aria-hidden="true"><span>↓</span></div>

              <%# 2. What came out. The hero of the panel. %>
              <div class="lb-why-result">
                <span class="lb-why-sw"
                      id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'wr', l.colour.hex,
                            'background:' + h.css.color(l.colour.hex)) %>"></span>
                <div>
                  <div class="lb-why-name"><%= l.colour.name %></div>
                  <div class="lb-why-hex"><%= l.colour.hex %></div>
                </div>
              </div>

              <%# 3. Every component's citation, each tied back to its anchor
                  by the same chip, and attributed to its section. %>
              <div class="lb-why-evidence">
                <% l.colour.components.forEach(function(c) { %>
                  <div class="lb-why-ev">
                    <div class="lb-why-lbl">
                      <i id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'wd', c.anchorHex,
                               'background:' + h.css.color(c.anchorHex)) %>"></i>
                      <span><%= c.emotion %></span>
                    </div>
                    <q><%= h.unquote(c.evidence) %></q>
                    <%# Trimmed: a DOAJ heading is a full article title, and
                        one running to two lines put that panel's source line
                        below every other panel's. %>
                    <span class="lb-why-sec"><%= h.shortSection(c.section) %></span>
                  </div>
                <% }) %>
              </div>

              <div class="lb-why-prov lb-cite"><%= l.colourSource %></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        <% } %>


      </div>

    </article>
  <% }) %>

  <%# ══ SEASON APPARATUS ══════════════════════════════════ %>

  <%#
    ── THE SEASON ON ONE LINE ────────────────────────────

    Every book of the season on a shared axis, twice: where each one fell in
    the season's own calendar, and where each one falls across the span of
    when they were written.

    This replaces three little bands inside every look. Those compared a
    book's setting against its author's life, and for almost every book two
    of the three were unknown — one tick and no comparison, a diagram of
    missing data. A season-wide line has the opposite property: it gets more
    interesting the more books are on it.
  %>
  <% if (book.timeline.read.marks.length >= 2) { %>
    <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-section-head">
        <div class="lb-label">THE SEASON, ON ONE LINE</div>
        <div class="lb-data"><%= book.timeline.read.marks.length %> FINISHED</div>
      </div>

      <%#
        A label per book, stacked into lanes so that two books finished in
        the same week do not print their numbers on top of each other. The
        marks keep their true positions; only the labels move.
      %>
      <div class="lb-tl">
        <div class="lb-tl-side">
          <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">READ</div>
          <div class="lb-cite"><%= book.timeline.read.from.slice(0, 4) %></div>
        </div>
        <div class="lb-tl-track">
          <% book.timeline.read.months.forEach(function(m) { %>
            <i class="lb-tl-month"
               id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'tm', book.season.code + '-' + m.label + '-' + Math.round(m.pct),
                     'left:' + h.css.pct(m.pct)) %>"></i>
          <% }) %>
          <% book.timeline.read.marks.forEach(function(m) { %>
            <a class="lb-tl-mark lb-tl-lane-<%= m.lane %>" href="/work/<%= m.workId %>"
               title="<%= m.title %> · <%= m.at %>"
               id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'tr', book.season.code + '-r' + m.n,
                     'left:' + h.css.pct(m.pct) +
                     (m.colour ? ';--tick:' + h.css.color(m.colour) : '')) %>">
              <span class="lb-tl-stem"></span>
              <span class="lb-tl-n"><%= String(m.n).padStart(2, '0') %></span>
            </a>
          <% }) %>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="lb-tl-axis">
        <span class="lb-tl-side"></span>
        <div class="lb-tl-scale lb-cite">
          <% book.timeline.read.months.forEach(function(m) { %><span><%= m.label %></span><% }) %>
        </div>
      </div>

      <% if (book.timeline.written) { %>
        <div class="lb-tl lb-tl--second">
          <div class="lb-tl-side">
            <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">WRITTEN</div>
            <div class="lb-cite"><%= book.timeline.written.to - book.timeline.written.from %> YRS</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lb-tl-track">
            <% book.timeline.written.marks.forEach(function(m) { %>
              <a class="lb-tl-mark lb-tl-lane-<%= m.lane %>" href="/work/<%= m.workId %>"
                 title="<%= m.title %> · <%= m.year %>"
                 id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'tw', book.season.code + '-w' + m.n,
                       'left:' + h.css.pct(m.pct) +
                       (m.colour ? ';--tick:' + h.css.color(m.colour) : '')) %>">
                <span class="lb-tl-stem"></span>
                <span class="lb-tl-n"><%= String(m.n).padStart(2, '0') %></span>
              </a>
            <% }) %>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lb-tl-axis">
          <span class="lb-tl-side"></span>
          <div class="lb-tl-scale lb-cite lb-tl-scale--ends">
            <span><%= book.timeline.written.from %></span>
            <span><%= book.timeline.written.to %></span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <%#
          Books the second line cannot place, named rather than silently
          dropped. "Drawn from four of seven" says three are missing but not
          which — and the reason is fixable: the year is a field on the book
          page.
        %>
        <% if (book.timeline.written.unplaced.length) { %>
          <p class="lb-tl-unplaced lb-cite">
            NOT ON THIS LINE, NO PUBLICATION YEAR ON RECORD —
            <% book.timeline.written.unplaced.forEach(function(u, i) { %><a
              class="lb-cite" href="/work/<%= u.workId %>"><%= u.title.toUpperCase() %></a><%=
              i < book.timeline.written.unplaced.length - 1 ? ' · ' : '' %><% }) %>
          </p>
        <% } %>
      <% } %>
    </section>
  <% } %>

  <%# COLORWAY — the season's own colours, in the season's own proportions.
      Not sampled from jackets any more: each one is what the book was
      derived to leave a reader with, or what its reader said instead.

      §05 — three assignments or it does not run. A two-band colourway is
      not a palette, it is two books. %>
  <% if (book.colourway.length >= 2 && book.colouredCount >= 3) { %>
    <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-section-head">
        <div class="lb-label">COLORWAY</div>
        <div class="lb-data"><%= book.colourway.length %> ACROSS <%= book.colouredCount %> BOOKS</div>
      </div>
      <div class="lb-colourway">
        <% book.colourway.forEach(function(c) { %>
          <div class="lb-swatch">
            <span class="lb-chip"
                  id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'lbc', c.hex, 'background:' + h.css.color(c.hex)) %>"></span>
            <%# The word first. §05: colour never carries meaning alone, and
                on this page the name is the meaning. %>
            <div class="lb-swatch-name"><%= c.emotion %></div>
            <div class="lb-swatch-hex lb-data"><%= c.name %> · <%= c.count %></div>
          </div>
        <% }) %>
      </div>
      <p class="lb-cite">EACH COLOUR DERIVED FROM WHAT THE SOURCES SAY THE BOOK IS DOING, OR CHOSEN BY THE READER.</p>
    </section>
  <% } else if (book.monochrome) { %>
    <%# §05 — "All books same colour. Valid and interesting. Say so plainly."
        A monochrome season is a real result and it gets a sentence, not a
        suppressed panel. %>
    <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-section-head">
        <div class="lb-label">COLORWAY</div>
      </div>
      <div class="lb-colourway">
        <div class="lb-swatch">
          <span class="lb-chip"
                id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'lbc', book.monochrome.hex, 'background:' + h.css.color(book.monochrome.hex)) %>"></span>
          <div class="lb-swatch-name"><%= book.monochrome.emotion %></div>
          <div class="lb-swatch-hex lb-data"><%= book.monochrome.name %> · <%= book.monochrome.count %></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="lb-cite">EVERY BOOK IN THIS SEASON CAME OUT THE SAME COLOUR.</p>
    </section>
  <% } %>

  <%# ══ BACK MATTER ══════════════════════════════════════ %>

  <%# LINE SHEET — "The ugly-beautiful page. Wholesale catalogue energy."
      Rating as a bare numeral with no stars. %>
  <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
    <div class="lb-section-head">
      <div class="lb-label">LINE SHEET</div>
    </div>
    <div class="lb-sheet-wrap">
      <table class="lb-sheet">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>NO</th><th>TITLE</th><th>DESIGNER</th>
            <th class="num">YARDAGE</th><th>ACQUIRED</th><th>COMPLETED</th><th class="num">GRADE</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <% book.lineSheet.forEach(function(r) { %>
            <tr>
              <td class="quiet"><%= String(r.n).padStart(3, '0') %></td>
              <td><%= r.title %></td>
              <td class="quiet"><%= r.author || '' %></td>
              <td class="num"><%= r.yardage || '—' %></td>
              <td class="quiet"><%= r.acquired || '—' %></td>
              <td class="quiet"><%= r.completed || '—' %></td>
              <td class="num"><%= r.rating != null ? r.rating : '—' %></td>
            </tr>
          <% }) %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# DEADSTOCK — "Goodreads treats abandonment as shameful; this puts it on
      the wall." §05 asked whether it should be opt-in; it ships on and the
      switch lives in settings, so a reader who finds it confronting rather
      than freeing can turn it off without losing the rest. %>
  <% if (book.deadstock.length) { %>
    <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-section-head">
        <div class="lb-label">DEADSTOCK</div>
        <div class="lb-data"><%= book.deadstock.length %> NOT PRODUCED</div>
      </div>
      <div class="lb-deadstock">
        <% book.deadstock.forEach(function(d) { %>
          <div class="lb-dead">
            <h3 class="lb-dead-title"><%= d.title %></h3>
            <p class="lb-look-author"><%= d.author || '' %></p>
            <p class="lb-dead-stamp">
              <% if (d.abandoned_page) { %>ABANDONED AT P. <%= d.abandoned_page %>.<% } %>
              NOT PRODUCED.
            </p>
          </div>
        <% }) %>
      </div>
    </section>
  <% } %>

  <%# THE PULP — "Lang shredded his own archive and made sculpture from the
      remains … Books are literally pulp."

      Assembled from the reader's own notes and therefore built only for
      them; the assembler returns nothing at all for anybody else. %>
  <% if (book.pulp) { %>
    <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
      <div class="lb-section-head">
        <div class="lb-label">THE PULP</div>
        <div class="lb-data"><%= book.pulp.length %> FRAGMENTS, INTERLEAVED</div>
      </div>
      <p class="lb-pulp">
        <% book.pulp.forEach(function(f) { %><span class="lb-shred"><%= f.text %></span><% }) %>
      </p>
      <p class="lb-cite">
        FROM NOTES WRITTEN BY THIS ACCOUNT. NOT SHOWN TO ANYONE ELSE.
      </p>
    </section>
  <% } %>

  <%# CREDITS — "Authors as designers. Translators as pattern cutters.
      Cover designer named. Publisher as the house."

      And here, finally, the reader's name. §02: it goes in the credits,
      like a house does. %>
  <section class="lb-section lb-assemble">
    <div class="lb-section-head">
      <div class="lb-label">CREDITS</div>
      <div class="lb-data">@<%= username %></div>
    </div>

    <div class="lb-grid">
      <div class="lb-c8">
        <div class="lb-credit-block">
          <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">DESIGN</div>
          <% book.credits.designers.forEach(function(d) { %>
            <div class="lb-credit-row">
              <span class="lb-credit-name"><%= d.name %></span>
              <span class="lb-cite"><%= d.title.toUpperCase() %></span>
            </div>
          <% }) %>
        </div>

        <% if (book.credits.cutters.length) { %>
          <div class="lb-credit-block">
            <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">PATTERN CUTTING</div>
            <% book.credits.cutters.forEach(function(c) { %>
              <div class="lb-credit-row">
                <span class="lb-credit-name"><%= c.name %></span>
                <span class="lb-cite"><%= c.title.toUpperCase() %> · <%= c.source %></span>
              </div>
            <% }) %>
          </div>
        <% } %>

        <% if (book.credits.jackets.length) { %>
          <div class="lb-credit-block">
            <div class="lb-label lb-label--quiet">JACKET</div>
            <% book.credits.jackets.forEach(function(c) { %>
              <div class="lb-credit-row">
                <span class="lb-credit-name"><%= c.name %></span>
                <span class="lb-cite"><%= c.title.toUpperCase() %> · <%= c.source %></span>
              </div>
            <% }) %>
          </div>
        <% } %>
      </div>

    </div>

    <%# The colophon is gone. It printed the reference number, the typefaces,
        where the subject headings came from, how many histories were
        documented, that the boards were built by hand, and a link back to
        the season — six lines of apparatus about the catalogue, at the foot
        of a catalogue about books. A colophon is a printer's note to other
        printers; nobody reading their own season needs to be told which
        fonts it is set in. Each card already carries its own source line,
        which is where sourcing belongs. %>
  </section>

  <%# CAMPAIGN — "One sentence from the season. White on black. Full bleed.
      No image. This is the share card." §00's two precedents meet here: the
      1998 digital-first release and the text-only billboards. %>
  <% if (book.campaign) { %>
    <section class="lb-campaign lb-assemble">
      <p><%= book.campaign.text %></p>
      <% if (book.campaign.from) { %>
        <p class="lb-cite"><%= book.campaign.from.toUpperCase() %></p>
      <% } %>
    </section>
  <% } %>
  <%#
    ── THREE TO FOLLOW (recommendation spec §07) ─────────

    Not "more of the same". Nearest-neighbours-to-the-centroid returns what
    the season already contained, which the reader has just finished; each
    slot instead stands in a different, defined relation to the season's
    shape — its centre, its gap, and its outlier's thread.

    Every rationale is a template with arithmetic dropped in. No model wrote
    a word of this page.

    A slot with no defensible answer prints the hatch rather than a worse
    book, and empty slots are common by design: four seasons have three or
    fewer books. The hatch has to look composed, exactly as the colourway
    voids do.
  %>
  <% if (locals.complements && complements.some(function(c){ return c.work_id; })) { %>
    <section class="lb-page lb-comp">
      <div class="lb-runhead">
        <span class="lb-lbl mono-s">COMPLEMENTS</span>
        <span class="lb-lbl mono-s"><%= (book.meta.short || book.season.code).toUpperCase().replace(/^(SS|AW)/, '$1 ') %></span>
      </div>

      <h2 class="lb-comp-h">Three to follow</h2>
      <p class="lb-comp-dek">
        Chosen against the shape of this season, from the books already on the
        shelf. Each stands in a different relation to what was read.
      </p>

      <div class="lb-three">
        <% complements.forEach(function(c) { %>
          <div class="lb-slot<%= c.work_id ? '' : ' is-void' %>">
            <div class="lb-slot-no mono-s">
              <span><%= c.slot === 'near' ? 'The near' : c.slot === 'absence' ? 'The absence' : 'The thread' %></span>
              <b><%= c.no %></b>
            </div>

            <% if (!c.work_id) { %>
              <%# The hatch. A slot with nothing defensible in it says so and
                  is not filled. %>
              <span class="lb-slot-jk lb-slot-hatch" aria-label="No defensible book for this slot"></span>
              <div class="lb-slot-t">Nothing here</div>
              <div class="lb-slot-by mono-s">NO BOOK ON THE SHELF ANSWERS THIS</div>
            <% } else { %>
              <%# The lookbook's own jacket, not h.frame(): this page links
                  lookbook.css alone, which carries no `.frame` rules at all.
                  Using the shared helper here rendered its fallback as raw
                  link text under an unconstrained image. %>
              <a class="lb-slot-jk lb-jacket-plate" href="/work/<%= c.work_id %>">
                <span class="lb-jacket-galley" aria-hidden="true">
                  <span class="lb-galley-title"><%= h.shortTitle(c.title, { max: 60 }) %></span>
                  <span class="lb-galley-author"><%= (c.author || '').toUpperCase() %></span>
                </span>
                <% if (c.cover_cache_key || c.cover_url) { %>
                  <img class="lb-jacket" src="<%= h.coverSrc(c) %>" alt="" loading="lazy" data-cover>
                <% } %>
              </a>
              <a class="lb-slot-t" href="/work/<%= c.work_id %>"><%= c.title %></a>
              <div class="lb-slot-by mono-s">
                <%= (c.author || 'Unknown').toUpperCase() %><% if (c.year) { %> · <%= c.year %><% } %>
              </div>

              <div class="lb-why">
                <%# The rationale is one template with the arithmetic marked
                    for emphasis. It is split on the marker rather than
                    interpolated as HTML, so every segment stays escaped and
                    no rationale can ever carry markup. %>
                <p class="lb-rsn"><%
                  c.rationale.split('**').forEach(function(part, i) {
                    if (i % 2) { %><b><%= part %></b><% } else { %><%= part %><% }
                  }) %></p>

                <% if (c.neighbourBooks.length) { %>
                  <div class="lb-bec mono-s">CLOSEST TO WHAT YOU FINISHED</div>
                  <%# Jackets, not titles. Three covers are read at a glance;
                      three truncated titles are a list to be parsed. %>
                  <div class="lb-nbrs">
                    <% c.neighbourBooks.forEach(function(n) { %>
                      <a class="lb-n lb-jacket-plate" href="/work/<%= n.work_id %>"
                         title="<%= n.title %><%= n.author ? ' · ' + n.author : '' %>">
                        <span class="lb-jacket-galley" aria-hidden="true">
                          <span class="lb-galley-title"><%= h.shortTitle(n.title, { max: 28 }) %></span>
                        </span>
                        <% if (n.cover_cache_key || n.cover_url) { %>
                          <img class="lb-jacket" src="<%= h.coverSrc(n) %>" alt="" loading="lazy" data-cover>
                        <% } %>
                      </a>
                    <% }) %>
                  </div>
                <% } %>

                <%# How much each source of text agreed — the audit made
                    visible. Only the marked kind ranked this book; the
                    others are shown because seeing that the criticism and
                    the plot summaries agree is the insight, and seeing that
                    they do not is equally one. %>
                <% if (c.kinds.length) { %>
                  <%# "AGREEMENT BY SOURCE" named the mechanism. What a
                      reader wants to know is which descriptions of the book
                      actually matched, and how strongly — so the heading
                      says that and the figures are percentages, which people
                      read, rather than cosines, which they do not. %>
                  <div class="lb-bec mono-s">HOW STRONGLY EACH MATCHED</div>
                  <div class="lb-kinds">
                    <%
                      var SOURCE = { 'text-plot': 'Plot summary',
                                     'text-criticism': 'Criticism',
                                     'text-blurb': 'Publisher blurb',
                                     'colour-components': 'Colour' };
                    %>
                    <% c.kinds.forEach(function(k) { %>
                      <div class="lb-kd<%= k.lead ? ' is-lead' : '' %>">
                        <span><%= SOURCE[k.kind] || k.kind %></span>
                        <%# The width arrives as a CUSTOM PROPERTY, not as a
                            width: the stylesheet animates 0 → var(--w) on
                            hover, and a nonced rule setting `width` outright
                            would paint the bar full before anyone looked. %>
                        <span class="lb-tr"><i id="<%= h.rule(styleRules, 'kd',
                          c.work_id + '-' + k.kind, '--w:' + h.css.pct(Math.round(k.cos * 100))) %>"></i></span>
                        <span class="lb-v"><%= Math.round(k.cos * 100) %>%</span>
                      </div>
                    <% }) %>
                  </div>
                  <%# Why this book, in the arithmetic that chose it.
                      This replaced "RANKED ON PLOT · THE REST ARE
                      AGREEMENTS, NOT INPUTS", which described the machinery
                      rather than the book and meant nothing to anyone who
                      had not read the spec. Every number here is measured,
                      and no model wrote the sentence — it is a template
                      with the figures dropped in, same as the rationale. %>
                  <% if (c.detail) { %>
                    <p class="lb-detail"><%= c.detail %></p>
                  <% } %>
                <% } %>
              </div>
            <% } %>
          </div>
        <% }) %>
      </div>

    </section>
  <% } %>
</main>
<%# §13.1 — the bruise, the timeline bars and the swatches are the only
    computed values on this page, and they arrive as real CSS rules under
    the response nonce. There is not one style attribute in this template. %>
<% if (locals.styleRules && styleRules.length) { %>
<style nonce="<%= nonce %>"><%- styleRules.join('') %></style>
<% } %>
</body>
</html>
